<h2>The seizure and the stalled trial</h2>
<p>On 10 February 2026, police intercepted a car with five occupants near Ayyampalayam, Tiruppur. The boot held a cattle-feed sack of dried ganja, weighed at the spot at about 22.200 kg in a single weighment. The petitioner — an 18-year-old student with no prior case, per his counsel — was, on the FIR&rsquo;s version, seated in the middle of the rear seat; the prosecution&rsquo;s counter placed him more centrally, alleging he drove the car, held the key, opened the boot and had hired the vehicle by pledging a co-accused&rsquo;s two-wheeler. He was arrested the same day under Sections 8(c) read with 20(b)(ii)(C), 25 and 29(1) of the NDPS Act, and by the time the High Court heard his second bail petition under Section 483 BNSS he had spent roughly nine months in custody. His first plea (Crl.O.P. No. 10343 of 2026) had been dismissed on 8 July 2026; charges were framed on 3 November 2026, and none of the sixteen cited witnesses had been examined.</p>

<h2>The weighment point</h2>
<p>The defence argument was arithmetical and statutory at once: the 22.200 kg figure was a single weighment of a mixed mass that included stalks — which fall outside the definition of ganja in Section 2(iii)(b) — along with leaves and seeds not shown to have accompanied the fruiting or flowering tops. Strip out what the statute excludes, and the true &ldquo;ganja&rdquo; weight may fall below the 20 kg commercial threshold, taking Section 37&rsquo;s twin conditions out of play.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The definition controls.</strong> Only the flowering or fruiting tops count as ganja; seeds and leaves count only when accompanying the tops, and stalks never do.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The burden sits with the prosecution.</strong> Where the seizure is weighed as one undifferentiated mass, it is for the prosecution to show that the statutory substance itself crosses the threshold.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The record did not show it.</strong> Both the FIR and the counter affidavit described stalks in the bulk, and the weighment never separated them.</div>
</div>
<p>Justice N. Ramesh was careful with the finding&rsquo;s edges: &ldquo;I do not hold that the quantity is below the commercial quantity,&rdquo; the order records — but &ldquo;there is a real possibility that the ganja, excluding the stalks&rdquo; falls below the threshold, and therefore, &ldquo;On the present record, the rigour of Section 37 of the Act is not shown to be attracted.&rdquo; The court drew on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s order in <em>Anand Dewar v. State of Chhattisgarh</em> (stated weight may include packaging) and the Madurai Bench&rsquo;s decision in <em>Ganesan v. State</em> (stalks must be excluded where the recovery weighs in the 21&ndash;25 kg band), treating the Andhra Pradesh decision in <em>Killo Subbarao</em> as persuasive. Conscious possession and knowledge were left strictly to trial; the petitioner&rsquo;s age, clean record and nine months of custody, with the trial yet to begin in earnest, completed the balance.</p>

<h2>Where the line now runs</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Seizure scenario</th><th>Bail consequence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Separated weighment showing tops alone above 20 kg</td><td>Section 37 twin conditions apply with full force.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Single weighment of mixed mass marginally above 20 kg, stalks visible on the record</td><td>Threshold not shown to be crossed; ordinary bail parameters govern.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Mixed mass far above the threshold (no realistic arithmetic brings it below 20 kg)</td><td>The stalks argument loses traction; Section 37 will ordinarily apply.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>In borderline ganja seizures, obtain the seizure mahazar and FSL papers early and check whether stalks, seeds and loose leaves were weighed with the tops — the composition of the weighed mass is a bail argument, not just a trial argument.</p>
  <p>The argument is strongest in the band just above the notified threshold; it is an argument about proof of quantity, not a defence on merits, and courts will say so expressly.</p>
  <p>Expect strict conditions where such bail is granted — daily marking, surety requirements, address affidavits — because the court is consciously preserving the trial&rsquo;s integrity while declining to apply Section 37 on an unproven threshold.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This is an informational case note. The order discussed arises from Tamil Nadu; its logic rests on the NDPS Act&rsquo;s definitions and is regularly invoked in other High Courts, but each case turns on its own seizure record.</div>
